 ('Lowest range', 'Lower range', 'Near forecast', 'Higher range', 'Highest range'): 1, ('Below $100 billion', '$100 billion to below $300 billion', '$300 billion to below $500 billion', '$500 billion to below $700 billion', '$700 billion or more'): 1, ('Below $650 billion', '$650 billion to below $700 billion', '$700 billion to below $790 billion', '$790 billion to below $850 billion', '$850 billion or more'): 1, ('Below $400 billion', '$400 billion to below $500 billion', '$500 billion to below $700 billion', '$700 billion to below $800 billion', '$800 billion or more'): 1, ('Below $450 billion', '$450 billion to below $550 billion', '$550 billion to below $650 billion', '$650 billion to below $750 billion', '$750 billion or more'): 1, ('Below $800 billion', '$800 billion to below $900 billion', '$900 billion to below $1 trillion', '$1 trillion to below $1.1 trillion', '$1.1 trillion or more'): 1, ('Below 4 GW', '4 GW to below 5 GW', '5 GW to below 10 GW', '10 GW to below 12 GW', '12 GW or more'): 1})\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r72b.stream:57:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_29\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \\\"python3 -B -c 'import sqlite3,glob,re\\nfor f in sorted(glob.glob('\\\\\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-?/prod/views.sqlite'\\\\\\\\'')):\\n c=sqlite3.connect('\\\\\\\\''file:'\\\\\\\\''+f+'\\\\\\\\''?mode=ro&immutable=1'\\\\\\\\'',uri=True)\\n print(f)\\n pat=re.compile(r'\\\\\\\\''.{0,150}(?:capex|hyperscaler|capital spending|infrastructure spending|capital expenditure).{0,200}'\\\\\\\\'',re.I)\\n found=[]\\n for vid,jid,day,reason,why in c.execute(\\\\\\\"select j.view_id,j.judgement_id,j.publication_day,j.reasons,j.why_changed from judgements j join view_keys k using(view_id) where k.measure in ('\\\\\\\\''event'\\\\\\\\'','\\\\\\\\''policy_event'\\\\\\\\'')\\\\\\\"):\\n  for m in pat.finditer((reason or '\\\\\\\\'''\\\\\\\\'')+'\\\\\\\\'' '\\\\\\\\''+(why or '\\\\\\\\'''\\\\\\\\'')):\\n   if re.search(r'\\\\\\\\''\\\\\\\\\\\"'$|billion|trillion|rung [345]|range|band|position [345]'\\\"'\\\\\\\\'',m[0],re.I):\\n    found.append((vid,jid,day,m[0]))\\n print(len(found));print(*found[:18],sep='\\\\\\\\''\\\\\\\\n'\\\\\\\\'')\\n'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-1/prod/views.sqlite\\n12\\n('v20', 499, '2026-08-13', 'undary to rung 1. The rung remains Yes and the reported $350 billion discussion is not a new independent confirmation. However, the updated v6 and v7 capex theme conclusions strengthen the demand backdrop supporting the same forecast; the repeated reports themselves do not change it.')\\n('v25', 548, '2026-08-15', ' outlets are treated as one claim, not independent confirmation. The rung and the core $350 billion financing-report basis are unchanged. The updated hyperscaler capex conclusions add relevant support for the scale of chip demand behind the proposed financing, changing the supporting rationale without changing the forecast rung.')\\n('v31', 653, '2026-08-19', 'argely restate the same plan and do not establish closing or provide a closing schedule, so the forecast remains uncertain rather than proven. The AI-capex themes modestly support project execution; the chip-export-control themes do not bear on this investment. The rung and the key supporting fact remain the same: NVIDIA announced the $1.5 billion plan,')\\n('v30', 737, '2026-08-23', 'ompletion. The repeated reports add no independent closing information, and the expected chip purchases provide only modest strategic support. The AI-capex themes modestly reinforce the project context but do not change the binary forecast. The forecast remains rung 1 with the same implied $1.5 billion completion by December 31, 2026. The additional rep')\\n('v17', 762, '2026-08-24', 'd NAVER’s expansion plan, do not establish that the investment has closed or that the financing condition will be met by the deadline. The broader AI-capex outlook supports the project’s prospects somewhat, but does not remove this specific closing condition. The rung and key supporting facts are unchanged: the planned $1 billion investment remains cond')\\n('v10', 865, '2026-08-28', 'ta-center capex at rung 4 and 2027 capex at rung 4, strengthening the contextual support for Firmus financing and changing the supporting outlook, without changing the implied completion date.')\\n('v29', 1022, '2026-09-03', 'artnership remains at the MOU stage with limited detail, plus a reported potential $125 billion backstop whose details are not final. Updated 2026–27 hyperscaler capex conclusions also strengthen the demand backdrop. These change the supporting risk and demand picture without moving the forecast across the binary rung boundary.')\\n('v29', 1037, '2026-09-04', 'eing negotiated and NVIDIA cautioned that the MOUs may not lead to definitive agreements, but roughly a year remains to complete them. The updated AI-capex themes also support continuing demand for infrastructure financing. These factors leave completion by the deadline as the more likely outcome, without treating the $500 billion target as proof that a')\\n('v29', 1037, '2026-09-04', 'greements are complete. The rung remains Yes, but the updated AI-capex themes add a stronger demand backdrop than the previous judgement had, changing the key support for the same forecast. Recent filings and reports reiterate that the arrangements remain MOUs or prelim')\\n('v10', 1049, '2026-09-04', 'O later in 2026, makes completion by the deadline more likely than non-completion, though the reported interest is not a confirmed commitment. The AI-capex themes modestly support the commercial context, but do not establish that the investment will close. The rung and its key transaction facts are unchanged: the same report of approximately US$500 mill')\\n('v31', 1060, '2026-09-04', \\\"nce now includes SB Energy's reported $3.2 billion first-half 2026 net loss and $139 million revenue, mostly from its legacy business. The updated AI-capex outlook also supports the project backdrop; neither changes the most likely completion outcome.\\\")\\n('v10', 1080, '2026-09-05', 'fact remain as in the previous conclusion: the specific but unconfirmed report of about US$500 million of interest tied to a 2026 IPO. The updated AI-capex themes reinforce the commercial context but do not change the implied outcome or rung.')\\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/views.sqlite\\n4\\n('v3', 175, '2026-07-20', ' The reported up-to-$500 billion is the possible full-build cost, not the size or terms of NVIDIA’s guarantee. The rung remains 2, but the updated AI-capex theme conclusion reinforces the forecast that the lease-related guarantee may proceed; it does not change the implied binary outcome or establish a guarantee amount.')\\n('v31', 1024, '2026-09-01', 'y still make completion by the deadline more likely than non-completion, so the binary rung does not change. The rung remains Yes, but the updated AI-capex themes add relevant support for the scale and continuation of the data-center buildout. They do not establish that the specific $1.5 billion investment has closed or change the implied completion out')\\n('v31', 1209, '2026-09-08', 'ut. The implied outcome therefore remains on the Yes side of the binary boundary. The rung and the core completion forecast are unchanged, but the AI-capex themes were updated since the prior judgement and now provide additional support for execution of the project. The specific $3 billion report is not a new supporting fact relative to the prior conclu')\\n('v31', 1463, '2026-09-16', 'and project-investment figures are not themselves evidence that this specific investment has been paid. The rung remains Yes, but the updated 2027 AI-capex theme adds support for the underlying data-center build and thus for completion of the investment; the report of a $3 billion agreement is part of the existing support, not a newly established transf')\\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-3/prod/views.sqlite\\n5\\n('v21', 468, '2026-08-11', 'reement to be signed; the evidence therefore supports Yes as the forecast, not as an already-proven outcome. The rung remains Yes, but the updated AI-capex themes reinforce the infrastructure-demand backdrop supporting the forecast. The newer $125 billion figure is an option across potential deals, not a confirmed Ohio-specific guarantee; repeated repor')\\n('v36', 799, '2026-08-25', 'e past the binary rung boundary. The rung remains Yes, and the shipment-testing evidence is unchanged. The updated 2027 AI-capex theme now points to spending around $1 trillion, refreshing and strengthening the demand support for the same forecast without supplying a shipment date.')\\n('v34', 945, '2026-08-30', ' or delay. Completion is still a forecast, not an established fact, and the repeated reports do not independently confirm a transfer of funds. The AI-capex themes support the project backdrop but do not establish completion. The rung and implied outcome are unchanged from the previous conclusion. The evidence reiterates the $1.5 billion plan and the pre')\\n('v34', 1382, '2026-09-12', 'ot confirm that funds have transferred or clarify how it relates to the original $1.5 billion, but it strengthens the commitment case; the updated AI-capex outlook also modestly supports execution. The implied outcome remains on the Yes side of the binary boundary. The rung and implied completion outcome are unchanged, but the updated 2027 AI-capex outl')\\n('v34', 1498, '2026-09-15', 'larger commitment, but its mention that SB Energy is seeking a Nasdaq listing to fund the project also leaves execution and timing unresolved. The AI-capex outlook supports the project backdrop, not proof of completion. The rung remains Yes, but e4452 adds a newly reported $3 billion investment agreement and Nasdaq-listing funding context, changing the ')\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r67.stream:50:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_25\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n -i 'product (that |is )?(has |hasn.t |had |is )?not (yet |already )?shipp|product with no shipments|not-yet shipping|volume in hand' docs/system-design docs/prd docs/DECISIONS-log.md | head -30 | cut -c1-950\\nnl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11342,11368p'\\nrg -n 're-send|same.*lines|singletons|gain' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | head -22\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"docs/DECISIONS-log.md:3072:- What reader B does that judging-v6 did not, read from B's answers on the questions that differed (`judgement-gold/reconcile-v1.md`, `gold-v1.json`): carries the guided growth forward rather than annualising the last quarter flat (Data Center, total revenue); discounts a management target for a product with no shipments by default (Vera CPU: two selling quarters, four million units at $5,000, the company's own choice of another CPU); sizes an announced but unsized commitment from its disclosed comparable (the Firmus backstop from Sharon's); derives a figure no one states from the accounting identities in the evidence (capex from operating less free cash flow); keeps linked conclusions consistent (China share against the H200 authorization theme). Written into `judging-v7.txt` as five clauses (v6 3,782 → 4,924 characters; reason: each clause is a behaviour of the frozen standard the judge lacked), named by `po\\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11674:  - Claude's closed list of \\\"volume in hand\\\" was rejected: it was wider and narrower than the standard at once.\\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11676:    puts the required volume in hand for the period\\\".\\n 11342\\t    - apply the rulings, in the reader brief and in code only where the structure decides them;\\n 11343\\t    - verify both reader families on the stored boundary replies;\\n 11344\\t    - re-run the registered blind test once with fresh writers and readers, then the paid re-send request.\\n 11345\\t\\n 11346\\t## D741 · 2026-11-22 · Alex rules the three reader questions of D740\\n 11347\\t\\n 11348\\t- **The questions** (after D740), each with its options; the recommended option is listed first:\\n 11349\\t  - 【增速参考】\\\"写手用公司指引的三季度 1080 亿算出“指引隐含增速约 12%”，参考它定四季度增速 13%，再套在自己预测的三季度 1136 亿上。进入收入计算的三季度数只有 1136 亿这一个。这算不算“同一季度用了两个数”的错？\\\" Options: 不算错 (推荐); 算错.\\n 11350\\t  - 【市场参照】\\\"分析师预期公司全年总收入 4110 亿，最近一个季度数据中心占 92.5%，乘出来约 3800 亿。能不能把这 3800 亿叫作“市场对数据中心全年的预期”？\\\" Options: 不能 (推荐); 能.\\n 11351\\t  - 【总开支当数据中心开支】\\\"问题问四大云厂商“数据中心资本开支”，原文只给了“总资本开支”（并说大部分投向 AI 基础设施）。一份答案直接拿总开支当数据中心开支，没扣非数据中心的部分。可以吗？\\\" Options: 必须写明扣多少 (推荐); 可以直接用.\\n 11352\\t- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-22): \\\"不算错 (推荐)\\\"; \\\"不能 (推荐)\\\"; \\\"必须写明扣多少 (推荐)\\\".\\n 11353\\t- **Decided (Alex):**\\n 11354\\t  1. **Not a defect:** a growth rate computed from guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate, when one figure\\n 11355\\t     supplies the quarter in the revenue calculation.\\n 11356\\t  2. **Not the market's reference:** consensus for a total × a past quarter's reported share. That figure is our comparison estimate.\\n 11357\\t     Only an outside figure for the member's own quantity (for example Morningstar's direct segment estimate), or a calculation that\\n 11358\\t     carries no past period's reported share or multiple to another period, is the market's reference.\\n 11359\\t  3. **A total used for a narrower quantity** (total capex for data-center capex) must state the deduction as its own figure, or give\\n 11360\\t     a supported bound that shows where the narrower quantity lands.\\n 11361\\t- **How they are applied (D738):**\\n 11362\\t  - Ruling 2's carried share is structural, so S5 refuses it.\\n 11363\\t  - Rulings 1 and 3 go in the request and in the reader brief, because they turn on what a label or an estimate means.\\n 11364\\t  - Then three steps:\\n 11365\\t    1. fix the S1 \\\"from … to …\\\" range (D740);\\n 11366\\t    2. check that both reader families apply the rulings the same way to the stored boundary replies;\\n 11367\\t    3. re-run the registered blind test once with fresh writers and readers.\\n 11368\\t\\n23:| bounds and their propagation (C2 bound direction, C3/C6 open ends) | a source's qualifier is checked against the quote and shown to readers; computed figures are points or ranges |\\n152:  than today's (17 figure fields against 23, shorter descriptions than v2).\\n253:- **INCOMPLETE** is judged against the inventory of 12 first replies and 24 readings plus the third readings due.\\n260:  answer, accepted or refused, against the shown passages. For each answer they record:\\n276:**A clean result** goes to Alex as the request for the paid re-send: the same 36 requests rebuilt in v13s, under a new decision with\\n300:  - only a complete run that meets every line goes to Alex for the paid re-send.\\n323:  - A clean re-read goes to Alex for the paid re-send, beside the third run's record and its disclosures.\\n329:  1. check quotes and calculations against the format rules;\\n341:  | K-unsupported | E's Claude reply, 140% growth that its own reasons argue against | supported = no |\\n348:  - A clean result goes to Alex for the paid re-send.\\n383:  - a clean result supports asking Alex to authorize the 36-request paid re-send.\\n436:    $0 writer test, then Alex for the paid re-send.\\n444:  `ac707fa9…`, checked by `blind5.py build` against every built system text). This is the wording after council round 64\\n497:  - Either outcome goes back to the council, and the paid re-send stays on hold.\\n498:  - Only a clean pass supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send.\\n518:  checked by `blind6.py build` against every built system text). Bullet 2's \\\"it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the\\n529:  - the same pass, fail and inconclusive lines.\\n534:  - a clean pass supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send;\\n538:  `blind5.py`, shared by `blind6.py`, gains three changes:\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r67.stream:61:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_31\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'def publish_decision|def _s5|reference_unavailable' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | tail -14\\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '456,491p'\\nnl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '3049,3054p;3069,3073p'\\nrg -n 'indirect|already selling|2\\\\\\\\.1x' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json | cut -c1-100\\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND6-RESULT.md | sed -n '36,49p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"185:        outs, refs, reason = outcome_for.get(v, []), reference_for.get(v, []), _text(a.get('reference_unavailable'))\\n188:                errors.append(('S7', f'{v}: an unresolved answer has why, rung and reference_unavailable null, and no figure marked for it'))\\n199:                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: needs exactly one of a figure marked as its reference or reference_unavailable'))\\n782:def publish_decision(family, calc, result):\\n   456\\t      wider);\\n   457\\t    - D749: decide the band before sizing, else unresolved, naming the unknown;\\n   458\\t    - D745's ruling: a justified unresolved answer is usable.\\n   459\\t  - Size: 4,540 → 4,794 characters, 910 → 961 tokens (o200k_base). The writer's request now carries the same routes the readers judge\\n   460\\t    by, and the decision order Alex approved.\\n   461\\t  - Checks at $0: cases 111 of 111, rehearsal 19 of 19, and the pinned v13 plan unchanged (`paid-plan.sha256`). The schema and the code\\n   462\\t    are unchanged.\\n   463\\t- **Scope** (`blind5.py`; run folder `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/`):\\n   464\\t  - cases S, E and P-v8, built from the repaired request;\\n   465\\t  - one fresh Claude subagent and one fresh read-only Codex session per case, 6 writers, each reading only `system.txt`, `user.txt` and\\n   466\\t    `schema.json`.\\n   467\\t- **Readers.** 8 blinded packets, shuffled X01–X08, with the key in `reader-key.json`:\\n   468\\t  - the 6 fresh replies;\\n   469\\t  - 2 controls carried unchanged from the targeted test, each shown with the request it was written for;\\n   470\\t  - each packet read by one fresh Claude and one fresh Codex reader under the D747 definitions.\\n   471\\t\\n   472\\t  Where they disagree, on support or on any defect, two adjudicators read the packet, one per family. The reasons behind every\\n   473\\t  settled vote are recorded by Claude as compatible or not (`reasons.json`). Matching votes with incompatible reasons go to\\n   474\\t  adjudication.\\n   475\\t\\n   476\\t  | Control | Built from | Expected reading |\\n   477\\t  |---|---|---|\\n   478\\t  | K-invented-cut | blind4 U05, E Codex: 90% of $40B with no order size or shipment pace | not supported |\\n   479\\t  | K-impossible-floor | blind4 U02, S Claude: \\\"more than $730B\\\" called a floor above its own $720B total | \\\"part above or added into its whole\\\" on F12, settled; its support is not scored |\\n   480\\t- **Pass:**\\n   481\\t  - every fresh S and E reply has a settled yes, on its forecast or on its unresolved reason;\\n   482\\t  - both fresh P-v8 replies have a settled yes on a forecast;\\n   483\\t  - every fresh reply is accepted and publishable by the code;\\n   484\\t  - no settled defect on a fresh reply;\\n   485\\t  - both controls read as expected.\\n   486\\t- **Otherwise (tally rules after council 64):**\\n   487\\t  - **Fail:**\\n   488\\t    - a fresh reply refused or held by the code (decided before any reading; an unreadable reply builds no packet);\\n   489\\t    - a fresh P-v8 reply that is not a forecast (decided from its shape alone);\\n   490\\t    - a settled no on a fresh reply;\\n   491\\t    - a settled defect on a fresh reply;\\n  3049\\t## D260 · 2026-11-02 · A judgement is a forecast against a reference: the rung the evidence in force makes most likely, judged against what the market expects; the answer states the reference and the implied outcome (judging-v6)\\n  3050\\t\\n  3051\\t- Alex, 2026-11-02, asked whether the judge's stance should stay an absolute-band lookup (the current reading) or be judged relative to expectations: \\\"门槛没有标准，按照你作为 hedge fund analyst 的认识来定\\\". Decided by Claude under that delegation: the definition below is Claude's analyst judgement, recorded as such, not Alex's words.\\n  3052\\t- Mechanism (arm 4 draw 1, all 356 judgements read, `research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/review-2026-11-02.md` §7 and `proof-judging-v6.txt`): 8 of 314 later judgements moved a rung and 320 of 356 reasons say \\\"remains\\\"; the FY2027 total revenue question was judged 12 times at neutral while its upward pushes grew from 1 to 35, every reason \\\"the $392 billion estimate is inside the band … does not establish $400 billion\\\"; the gross-margin judgement wrote \\\"leans toward rung 2 but does not warrant moving\\\"; the 2027 hyperscaler-capex theme stayed below the band management's own forecast names because the forecast \\\"is not fact\\\". The judging-v5 prompt asked the judge to \\\"be precise about what is established\\\" and said only fact can establish a rung: the model read \\\"established\\\" as the bar for leaving a rung and the consensus figure as the anchor. A second cause stays open: the ladder's neutral band is written at opening without naming what it compares against (PRD 06 ladder rule 2 asks for it); that is the extraction's rule and is not changed here.\\n  3053\\t- Decided, the analyst's rule: (1) the rung is a forecast, the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved; \\\"not established\\\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. (2) The reference is what the market expects: consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline; a consensus or guidance figure alone keeps the judgement at the reference's rung and, once other evidence has arrived, is never the reason to stay. (3) The judge derives the outcome the evidence implies (reported figures and guidance give a run-rate; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it), checks it against the dependencies' conclusions (a part cannot exceed its whole; a figure spanning several periods or products gives the question only its share), compares it with the rung boundaries and moves when it crosses one. (4) Weight follows tier and novelty: fact fixes what is known; party statements are the strongest forecast input; third-party views and rumours move the forecast in proportion to their specificity; a repeat, another outlet or an opinion with no new fact or calculation moves nothing. (5) The answer states `reference` and `implied_outcome` as required fields from `judging-v6` (`judging.schema_for`; code refuses an empty one; stored beside the rung in `judgements.reference` / `implied_outcome`; a ledger written before v6 gains the columns at its first write), so the D3 stance gold can read the reasoning, not only the rung. Written where the judgement is specified: PRD 06 State rules (`docs/system-design/new-facts.js` STATE_RULES, the generated block) and this record.\\n  3054\\t- Proof (`proof-judging-v6.txt`, `proof-judging-v6b.txt`: the last stored judgement request of 14 theses of arm 4 draw 1 re-sent once each under v6 and read beside the stored v5 answers, about a cent): every answer names the reference and derives the implied outcome. Six rungs moved against the stored ones: non-GAAP gross margin 3 → 2 (Q1 reported 75% and Q2 guided 75% sit on the rung-2 boundary), 2027 hyperscaler capex 4 → 5 (management's own \\\"more than $1 trillion\\\"), China accelerator share 4 → 5 (share \\\"fallen to zero\\\"), China H200 authorization No → Yes (a reported plan to permit named firms and reported progress in reviews, six months before the deadline), Data Center revenue 2 → 3 ($355–360 billion derived from Q1 and the total-revenue proxy, below the $360 billion boundary), Blackwell+Rubin revenue 2 → 1 in the first send by annualising a two-year \\\"$1 trillion visibility\\\" figure to $545 billion, a part above its whole: clause (3)'s consistency check was added for it and the re-send gives rung 2 ($320–360 billion, a share of the Data Center segment). Total revenue stays at 3 with the arithmetic shown (H1 $172.6 billion from Q1 and the Q2 guide; $400 billion needs more than $227 billion in H2). Prompt 2,453 → 3,782 characters: the request defined neither what a rung means nor a reference and its \\\"established\\\" wording forced the defect; the two sentences that carried it were cut. Configuration `poc-v90` (poc-v89 with judging-v6); the default stays `poc-v69`. Tests: `tests/test_stance_fields.py`. Measured by re-sent stored requests, not by an arm (Alex: \\\"不要再跑这些有的没的臂了\\\").\\n  3069\\t## D263 · 2026-11-02 · The judge is written to the frozen gold's own reasoning (judging-v7, poc-v91); a gap to the gold is closed in the judge, not re-measured\\n  3070\\t\\n  3071\\t- Alex, after the first score on the frozen gold was reported as the system being \\\"wrong\\\" on some questions: \\\"不是啊什么叫错？我不是和你说了就按照claude的标准来就好了嘛\\\". The gold is Claude's (reader B's) standard by D262; a disagreement is the distance between the judge and that standard, to be closed in the judge's rule, not a verdict to be measured again.\\n  3072\\t- What reader B does that judging-v6 did not, read from B's answers on the questions that differed (`judgement-gold/reconcile-v1.md`, `gold-v1.json`): carries the guided growth forward rather than annualising the last quarter flat (Data Center, total revenue); discounts a management target for a product with no shipments by default (Vera CPU: two selling quarters, four million units at $5,000, the company's own choice of another CPU); sizes an announced but unsized commitment from its disclosed comparable (the Firmus backstop from Sharon's); derives a figure no one states from the accounting identities in the evidence (capex from operating less free cash flow); keeps linked conclusions consistent (China share against the H200 authorization theme). Written into `judging-v7.txt` as five clauses (v6 3,782 → 4,924 characters; reason: each clause is a behaviour of the frozen standard the judge lacked), named by `poc-v91`; schema and code unchanged.\\n  3073\\t- Proof on the stored requests of the questions whose arm-4 judgement differed from the gold (`proof-judging-v7a.txt`, `proof-judging-v7.txt`; one send each, cents): total revenue rung 3 → 2 (about $421 billion from the guided growth on FY2026's base; gold $400–435 billion), Data Center 3 → 2 (about $372 billion; gold $370–400 billion), Vera CPU 1 → 2 ($12 to below $20 billion, the target discounted for the volume not in hand; gold $8–18 billion), DRAM prices 3 → 2 (about +15%; gold +10–35%), financing total stays 4 ($33.5–39.8 billion; gold $25–60 billion), the Ohio guarantee stays Yes; China share stays below 1% in draw 1, consistent with that draw's H200 theme judged No (the gold has the authorization likely and the share 1–8%; the consistency clause ties the share to the theme, so it moves with it). Six of the seven re-sent questions now land in the gold's band; the end-to-end agreement under judging-v7 needs a run on `poc-v91`, which is Alex's call. A placement gap seen on the way: the Vera CPU question never held the fall-launch statement (filed under the Rubin launch questions), so the judge could not count selling quarters from evidence; the default-discount clause covers it, the filing is a D2 class to count.\\n1:{\\\"figures\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"F1\\\",\\\"what\\\":{\\\"population\\\":\\\"NVIDIA\\\",\\\"measure\\\":\\\"revenue\\\",\\\"scope\\\":\\\"CPU\\\",\\\"period\\\":\\\"F\\n    36\\t## 2. The E record across the four runs (same case, same passages)\\n    37\\t\\n    38\\t| Run | Codex writer | Claude writer |\\n    39\\t|---|---|---|\\n    40\\t| third run (D742 request) | 85% realisation, $34B; settled no | 1.85x bracket, $37B; yes in re-read 2, split under D747 |\\n    41\\t| targeted test (D745) | 90% realisation, $36B; settled no | 1.8x × 90% share, $32.4B; split, then no under D747 |\\n    42\\t| D749 | unresolved, specific gap; settled yes | 2.1x in full, $42B, asserting \\\"already selling at volume\\\"; split |\\n    43\\t| D750 | unresolved, specific gap; settled yes | 1.75x bracket midpoint, $35B; settled no |\\n    44\\t\\n    45\\t- **The Codex writer.** The repair (D749, D750) moved it from invented cuts to a justified unresolved, twice.\\n    46\\t- **The Claude writer.** It forecasts every time, by a different route each time, and has never been settled supported under the\\n    47\\t  current definitions.\\n    48\\t- **S** converged over D749 and D750: all four fresh forecasts are supported. Only defect marks on one answer remain split.\\n    49\\t- **P-v8** has been supported throughout.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r45.stream:59:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_30\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \\\"python3 -B -c 'import sqlite3,json,pathlib,re,collections,decimal\\nD=decimal.Decimal\\ndef db(i,name='\\\\\\\\''views'\\\\\\\\''):\\n c=sqlite3.connect((pathlib.Path(f'\\\\\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-{i}/prod'\\\\\\\\'')/(name+'\\\\\\\\''.sqlite'\\\\\\\\'')).resolve().as_uri()+'\\\\\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\\\\\'',uri=True)\\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\\n return c\\ndef req(a,j):\\n c=a.execute('\\\\\\\\''select * from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\\\\\'',(j['\\\\\\\\''call_id'\\\\\\\\''],)).fetchone()\\n assert c and c['\\\\\\\\''request_sha256'\\\\\\\\'']==j['\\\\\\\\''request_sha256'\\\\\\\\''] and c['\\\\\\\\''purpose'\\\\\\\\'']=='\\\\\\\\''judge'\\\\\\\\'',dict(j)\\n d=json.loads(c['\\\\\\\\''request_json'\\\\\\\\''])\\n return d,json.loads(c['\\\\\\\\''answer_json'\\\\\\\\''])\\nEVID=re.compile(r'\\\\\\\\''\\\"'^- (e'\\\"\\\\\\\\d+) \\\\\\\\[.*?(?=\\\"'^- e'\\\"\\\\\\\\d+ \\\\\\\\[|\\\\\\\\Z)'\\\\\\\\'',re.M|re.S)\\ndef evidence(t):\\n b=t.split('\\\\\\\\''[Evidence in view under this thesis, '\\\\\\\\'',1)[1].split('\\\\\\\\''\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n['\\\\\\\\'',1)[0]\\n m=dict((x.group(1),x.group(0).strip()) for x in EVID.finditer(b))\\n assert len(m)==int(b.split()[0]),(len(m),b[:20])\\n return m\\ndef user(d):\\n return next(m['\\\\\\\\''content'\\\\\\\\''] for m in d['\\\\\\\\''messages'\\\\\\\\''] if m['\\\\\\\\''role'\\\\\\\\'']=='\\\\\\\\''user'\\\\\\\\'')\\nUNIT=r'\\\\\\\\''(?:trillion(?:s)?|billions?|millions?|bn|mm|[BTM]|%|percent(?:age points?)?)'\\\\\\\\''\\nNUM=r'\\\\\\\\''\\\\\\\\d+(?:,\\\\\\\\d{3})*(?:\\\\\\\\.\\\\\\\\d+)?'\\\\\\\\''\\nFIG=re.compile(rf'\\\\\\\\''(?<\\\"'!['\\\"\\\\\\\\w.])(?P<sign>[+−-])?\\\\\\\\\\\"'$?'\\\"\\\\\\\\s*(?P<lo>{NUM})(?:\\\\\\\\s*[-–—]\\\\\\\\s*\\\\\\\\\\\"'$?'\\\"\\\\\\\\s*(?P<hi>{NUM}))?\\\\\\\\s*(?P<unit>{UNIT})(?\\\"'![a-zA-Z0-9])'\\\"'\\\\\\\\'',re.I)\\nMULT={'\\\\\\\\''trillion'\\\\\\\\'':1000,'\\\\\\\\''trillions'\\\\\\\\'':1000,'\\\\\\\\''t'\\\\\\\\'':1000,'\\\\\\\\''billion'\\\\\\\\'':1,'\\\\\\\\''billions'\\\\\\\\'':1,'\\\\\\\\''bn'\\\\\\\\'':1,'\\\\\\\\''b'\\\\\\\\'':1,'\\\\\\\\''million'\\\\\\\\'':D('\\\\\\\\''.001'\\\\\\\\''),'\\\\\\\\''millions'\\\\\\\\'':D('\\\\\\\\''.001'\\\\\\\\''),'\\\\\\\\''mm'\\\\\\\\'':D('\\\\\\\\''.001'\\\\\\\\''),'\\\\\\\\''m'\\\\\\\\'':D('\\\\\\\\''.001'\\\\\\\\'')}\\ndef figures(t,unit):\\n out=[]\\n for m in FIG.finditer(t or '\\\\\\\\'''\\\\\\\\''):\\n  u=m['\\\\\\\\''unit'\\\\\\\\''].lower()\\n  if (u in MULT)\\\"'!=(unit=='\\\"'\\\\\\\\''USD'\\\\\\\\''):continue\\n  mult=MULT.get(u,1)\\n  if m['\\\\\\\\''sign'\\\\\\\\''] in ('\\\\\\\\''-'\\\\\\\\'','\\\\\\\\''−'\\\\\\\\''):mult=-mult\\n  for g in ('\\\\\\\\''lo'\\\\\\\\'','\\\\\\\\''hi'\\\\\\\\''):\\n   if m[g]:out.append(D(m[g].replace('\\\\\\\\'','\\\\\\\\'','\\\\\\\\'''\\\\\\\\''))*mult)\\n return out\\ndef snippets(ed):\\n return [re.split(r'\\\\\\\\''\\\\\\\\n    (?:It replaces|Opinion, not verified:)'\\\\\\\\'',b,1)[0].split('\\\\\\\\''] '\\\\\\\\'',1)[1] for b in ed.values()]\\ndef prior_text(t):\\n return t.split('\\\\\\\\''[Previous conclusion'\\\\\\\\'',1)[1].split('\\\\\\\\''\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAnswer per'\\\\\\\\'',1)[0].split('\\\\\\\\''] '\\\\\\\\'',1)[1]\\ndef tag(x,pattern): return bool(re.search(pattern,x or '\\\\\\\\'''\\\\\\\\'',re.I))\\ndef record(i):\\n v=db(i);a=db(i,'\\\\\\\\''archive'\\\\\\\\'')\\n return (v,a,v.execute(\\\\\\\"select j.*,k.unit from judgements j join view_keys k using(view_id) where k.unit in ('\\\\\\\\''USD'\\\\\\\\'','\\\\\\\\''percent'\\\\\\\\'') order by judgement_id\\\\\\\"))\\nfor i in (1,2,3):\\n v,a,rows=record(i)\\n C=collections.Counter(); first={}; prior={}; explicit=[]; matches={}\\n def tick(k,day):\\n  C[k]+=1\\n  first.setdefault(k,day)\\n def near(x,ys):\\n  return any(abs(x-y)<=D('\\\\\\\\''.005'\\\\\\\\'')*max(abs(x),abs(y),D('\\\\\\\\''.00000001'\\\\\\\\'')) for y in ys)\\n for j in rows:\\n  d,raw=req(a,j);t=user(d);e=evidence(t);u=j['\\\\\\\\''unit'\\\\\\\\'']; dy=j['\\\\\\\\''publication_day'\\\\\\\\''];id=j['\\\\\\\\''view_id'\\\\\\\\'']; key=(id,dy)\\n  assert all(raw[k]==j[k] for k in ('\\\\\\\\''rung'\\\\\\\\'','\\\\\\\\''reference'\\\\\\\\'','\\\\\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\\\\\'')) and set(raw['\\\\\\\\''cites'\\\\\\\\''])<=set(e), (i,j['\\\\\\\\''call_id'\\\\\\\\''])\\n  vals=figures(j['\\\\\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\\\\\''],u); ref=figures(j['\\\\\\\\''reference'\\\\\\\\''],u)\\n  s=snippets(e); shown=figures('\\\\\\\\''\\\\\\\\n'\\\\\\\\''.join(s),u); quotes=figures('\\\\\\\\''\\\\\\\\n'\\\\\\\\''.join(s1.split('\\\\\\\\''\\\\\\\\n    Quote: '\\\\\\\\'',1)[1] for s1 in s),u)\\n  tick('\\\\\\\\''all-'\\\\\\\\''+u,dy)\\n  if '\\\\\\\\''[Not shown:'\\\\\\\\'' in t:tick('\\\\\\\\''hidden'\\\\\\\\'',dy)\\n  if vals:\\n   tick('\\\\\\\\''parsed-'\\\\\\\\''+u,dy)\\n   if not near(vals[0],shown):tick('\\\\\\\\''lead no nearby-'\\\\\\\\''+u,dy)\\n   if not near(vals[0],quotes):tick('\\\\\\\\''lead no quote nearby-'\\\\\\\\''+u,dy)\\n   if not any(near(x,shown) for x in vals):tick('\\\\\\\\''no nearby at all-'\\\\\\\\''+u,dy)\\n   if any(not near(x,shown) for x in vals):tick('\\\\\\\\''at least one new-'\\\\\\\\''+u,dy)\\n  if id in prior:\\n   tick('\\\\\\\\''previous'\\\\\\\\'',dy)\\n   if ref:\\n    tick('\\\\\\\\''parsed-ref'\\\\\\\\'',dy)\\n    own=bool(prior[id]) and near(ref[0],prior[id][:1])\\n    no=not near(ref[0],shown)\\n    carried=tag(j['\\\\\\\\''reference'\\\\\\\\''],r'\\\\\\\\''\\\\\\\\b(?:previous|prior|carried)\\\\\\\\b'\\\\\\\\'')\\n    prev=near(ref[0],figures(prior_text(t),u))\\n    if own and no:tick('\\\\\\\\''own and no evidence'\\\\\\\\'',dy)\\n    if carried and no:tick('\\\\\\\\''carried and no evidence'\\\\\\\\'',dy)\\n    if carried and prev and no:\\n     tick('\\\\\\\\''carried prev no evidence'\\\\\\\\'',dy)\\n     explicit.append((id,dy,j['\\\\\\\\''call_id'\\\\\\\\''],j['\\\\\\\\''reference'\\\\\\\\'']))\\n    if carried and own and no:tick('\\\\\\\\''carried own no evidence'\\\\\\\\'',dy)\\n    if i==2 and id=='\\\\\\\\''v9'\\\\\\\\'' and dy in ('\\\\\\\\''2026-09-15'\\\\\\\\'','\\\\\\\\''2026-09-20'\\\\\\\\''):assert no and prev and (own if dy.endswith('\\\\\\\\''15'\\\\\\\\'') else carried)\\n  prior[id]=vals\\n  if (i,id,dy) in ((2,'\\\\\\\\''v9'\\\\\\\\'','\\\\\\\\''2026-09-20'\\\\\\\\''),(2,'\\\\\\\\''v14'\\\\\\\\'','\\\\\\\\''2026-09-20'\\\\\\\\''),(2,'\\\\\\\\''v29'\\\\\\\\'','\\\\\\\\''2026-09-20'\\\\\\\\''),(2,'\\\\\\\\''v6'\\\\\\\\'','\\\\\\\\''2026-09-19'\\\\\\\\''),(3,'\\\\\\\\''v41'\\\\\\\\'','\\\\\\\\''2026-09-20'\\\\\\\\'')):\\n   print('\\\\\\\\''control'\\\\\\\\'',i,id,dy,vals,[(str(x),near(x,shown)) for x in vals],ref)\\n print(i,dict(C),first)\\n print('\\\\\\\\''carried'\\\\\\\\'',explicit[:14],explicit[-4:])\\n'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"1 {'all-USD': 737, 'parsed-USD': 736, 'lead no nearby-USD': 345, 'lead no quote nearby-USD': 389, 'no nearby at all-USD': 27, 'at least one new-USD': 524, 'previous': 821, 'parsed-ref': 798, 'hidden': 611, 'all-percent': 103, 'parsed-percent': 103, 'at least one new-percent': 66, 'own and no evidence': 4, 'lead no nearby-percent': 42, 'lead no quote nearby-percent': 42, 'carried and no evidence': 17, 'carried prev no evidence': 16, 'carried own no evidence': 1} {'all-USD': '2026-07-01', 'parsed-USD': '2026-07-01', 'lead no nearby-USD': '2026-07-01', 'lead no quote nearby-USD': '2026-07-01', 'no nearby at all-USD': '2026-07-01', 'at least one new-USD': '2026-07-01', 'previous': '2026-07-02', 'parsed-ref': '2026-07-02', 'hidden': '2026-07-07', 'all-percent': '2026-07-08', 'parsed-percent': '2026-07-08', 'at least one new-percent': '2026-07-08', 'own and no evidence': '2026-07-16', 'lead no nearby-percent': '2026-07-18', 'lead no quote nearby-percent': '2026-07-18', 'carried and no evidence': '2026-07-26', 'carried prev no evidence': '2026-07-26', 'carried own no evidence': '2026-08-02'}\\ncarried [('v2', '2026-07-26', 1919, 'No market consensus or NVIDIA guidance for the FY2027 outstanding total is supplied. The closest quantified disclosed baseline is $8.38 billion from the $3.5 billion added guarantee and Sharon AI’s $4.88 billion backstop, which sits in rung 4; the previous carried estimate including Firmus was about $11.5 billion, rung 5.'), ('v8', '2026-07-27', 2159, 'The carried-forward market-reference proxy is about $372.5 billion, in rung 2 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).'), ('v8', '2026-07-28', 2315, 'The carried-forward estimate is about $372.5 billion, in rung 2 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).'), ('v8', '2026-07-29', 2429, 'The carried-forward market estimate is about $372.5 billion, which is rung 2 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).'), ('v8', '2026-07-30', 2541, 'The carried-forward market estimate is about $372.5 billion, in rung 2 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).'), ('v8', '2026-07-31', 2630, 'The carried-forward market estimate is about $372.5 billion, which sits in rung 2 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).'), ('v1', '2026-08-02', 2692, 'No aggregate market consensus or NVIDIA guidance is supplied, so there is no external reference figure to place on the ladder. The carried base case of roughly $10–11 billion is in rung 4.'), ('v8', '2026-08-02', 2694, 'Analyst consensus is the reference; no numeric consensus is supplied in the current evidence. The carried comparison places it below the implied outcome, most consistently on rung 2 ($350bn to below $400bn).'), ('v1', '2026-08-03', 2728, 'No aggregate market consensus or NVIDIA guidance for total FY2027 credit-support obligations is supplied. The carried base estimate of about $10–11 billion is the available reference and sits in rung 4; the reported $250 billion Ohio proposal is not itself an established reference for committed obligations.'), ('v1', '2026-08-05', 2808, 'No aggregate market consensus or NVIDIA guidance for total FY2027 credit-support commitments is supplied. The carried base estimate of about $10–11 billion is rung 4; the reported $250 billion Ohio guarantee headline, if fully committed, would be in rung 5, but is not the most reliable reference for the committed amount by the due date.'), ('v8', '2026-08-05', 2805, 'Analyst consensus; no numeric figure is supplied in this view. The carried comparison places the reference most consistently in rung 2 ($350bn to below $400bn).'), ('v1', '2026-08-06', 2871, 'No explicit market consensus or aggregate NVIDIA guidance is supplied. The carried non-Ohio estimate of about $10–11 billion is rung 4; the carried estimate including a discounted Ohio contribution is about $20–21 billion, rung 5. The reported $250 billion Ohio figure, if taken in full, would also be rung 5 but is not the reference forecast used here.'), ('v8', '2026-08-09', 2975, 'Analyst consensus, as described in the previous conclusion, is $350bn to below $400bn: rung 2. No exact consensus estimate is supplied.'), ('v8', '2026-08-25', 4589, \\\"The carried-forward analyst-consensus reference is $350 billion to below $400 billion, rung 2. Morningstar's FY2027 estimate of $361 billion is likewise rung 2.\\\")] [('v8', '2026-08-09', 2975, 'Analyst consensus, as described in the previous conclusion, is $350bn to below $400bn: rung 2. No exact consensus estimate is supplied.'), ('v8', '2026-08-25', 4589, \\\"The carried-forward analyst-consensus reference is $350 billion to below $400 billion, rung 2. Morningstar's FY2027 estimate of $361 billion is likewise rung 2.\\\"), ('v8', '2026-08-26', 5093, \\\"Analyst consensus carried forward from the previous judgement is $350 billion to below $400 billion, rung 2. Morningstar's $361 billion FY2027 estimate is also in rung 2.\\\"), ('v8', '2026-08-27', 5304, \\\"The carried-forward market consensus is $350 billion to below $400 billion, which is rung 2; Morningstar's specific $361 billion FY2027 estimate is also rung 2.\\\")]\\ncontrol 2 v6 2026-09-19 [Decimal('1190.00'), Decimal('730'), Decimal('800'), Decimal('1300.0')] [('1190.00', False), ('730', True), ('800', True), ('1300.0', True)] [Decimal('1080.00'), Decimal('900'), Decimal('1100.0')]\\ncontrol 2 v14 2026-09-20 [Decimal('419'), Decimal('81.6'), Decimal('96.2'), Decimal('113.5'), Decimal('110.16'), Decimal('127')] [('419', False), ('81.6', True), ('96.2', True), ('113.5', True), ('110.16', True), ('127', False)] [Decimal('411'), Decimal('400'), Decimal('450'), Decimal('215.9'), Decimal('405')]\\ncontrol 2 v9 2026-09-20 [Decimal('410'), Decimal('164'), Decimal('100'), Decimal('108'), Decimal('146')] [('410', False), ('164', True), ('100', True), ('108', True), ('146', False)] [Decimal('414'), Decimal('400'), Decimal('450')]\\ncontrol 2 v29 2026-09-20 [Decimal('53'), Decimal('25'), Decimal('18'), Decimal('3.5'), Decimal('10'), Decimal('2.5')] [('53', False), ('25', True), ('18', True), ('3.5', True), ('10', True), ('2.5', True)] [Decimal('70'), Decimal('50'), Decimal('75')]\\n2 {'all-USD': 664, 'parsed-USD': 664, 'lead no nearby-USD': 378, 'lead no quote nearby-USD': 378, 'no nearby at all-USD': 27, 'at least one new-USD': 522, 'previous': 823, 'parsed-ref': 764, 'hidden': 595, 'all-percent': 176, 'parsed-percent': 175, 'at least one new-percent': 112, 'carried and no evidence': 29, 'carried prev no evidence': 29, 'lead no nearby-percent': 69, 'lead no quote nearby-percent': 69, 'no nearby at all-percent': 15, 'own and no evidence': 9, 'carried own no evidence': 1} {'all-USD': '2026-07-01', 'parsed-USD': '2026-07-01', 'lead no nearby-USD': '2026-07-01', 'lead no quote nearby-USD': '2026-07-01', 'no nearby at all-USD': '2026-07-01', 'at least one new-USD': '2026-07-01', 'previous': '2026-07-02', 'parsed-ref': '2026-07-02', 'hidden': '2026-07-07', 'all-percent': '2026-07-17', 'parsed-percent': '2026-07-17', 'at least one new-percent': '2026-07-21', 'carried and no evidence': '2026-07-22', 'carried prev no evidence': '2026-07-22', 'lead no nearby-percent': '2026-07-22', 'lead no quote nearby-percent': '2026-07-22', 'no nearby at all-percent': '2026-07-22', 'own and no evidence': '2026-08-29', 'carried own no evidence': '2026-09-09'}\\ncarried [('v9', '2026-07-22', 1453, \\\"No full-year FY2027 Data Center consensus or company guidance is supplied. The available annual run-rate reference is about $372B (FY2026 Data Center revenue of $193.7B carried forward at Q1 FY2027's 92% year-over-year growth), which sits in rung 3. NVIDIA's approximately $91B Q2 total-revenue guidance, assuming no China Data Center revenue, is a near-term reference, not a full-year estimate.\\\"), ('v9', '2026-07-24', 1649, \\\"Approximately $372B, in rung 3, using FY2026 Data Center revenue of $193.7B carried forward at FY2027 Q1's 92% year-over-year growth rate; no separate consensus estimate is supplied.\\\"), ('v9', '2026-07-25', 1717, \\\"No explicit consensus estimate is supplied. The reference is about $372B, calculated as FY2026 Data Center revenue of $193.7B carried forward at FY2027 Q1's 92% year-over-year growth rate; this is rung 3.\\\"), ('v9', '2026-07-26', 1787, \\\"Market reference: about $372 billion, in rung 3, based on FY2026 Data Center revenue of $193.7 billion carried forward at FY2027 Q1's 92% year-over-year growth rate.\\\"), ('v9', '2026-07-27', 2013, 'About $372 billion, in rung 3, using FY2026 Data Center revenue of $193.7 billion carried forward at the 92% year-over-year growth reported for FY2027 Q1. No separate consensus figure is supplied.'), ('v9', '2026-08-17', 3608, \\\"About $372B, in rung 3: FY2026 Data Center revenue of $193.7B carried forward at FY2027 Q1's 92% year-over-year growth; this is the run-rate reference, not the forecast.\\\"), ('v9', '2026-08-18', 3747, 'Approximately $372B, the market expectation stated in the previous conclusion; that lies in rung 3 ($350B to below $400B).'), ('v9', '2026-08-19', 3874, 'Approximately $372B, in rung 3 ($350B to below $400B), as the market/reference estimate carried forward from FY2026 Data Center revenue at the reported FY2027 Q1 year-over-year growth rate.'), ('v9', '2026-08-21', 3993, 'Approximately $372B, as carried from the prior conclusion; that reference sits in rung 3 ($350B to below $400B).'), ('v9', '2026-08-22', 4029, 'Approximately $372B, based on FY2026 Data Center revenue of $193.7B carried forward at 92% growth; this reference is in rung 3.'), ('v9', '2026-08-24', 4283, 'The market reference carried from the previous judgement is approximately $372B, derived there from FY2026 Data Center revenue of $193.7B carried forward at the reported 92% growth rate; this sits in rung 3 ($350B to below $400B). No new FY2027 consensus figure is supplied.'), ('v9', '2026-08-25', 4437, 'The carried market expectation is approximately $372B for FY2027 Data Center revenue, which is rung 3 ($350B to below $400B).'), ('v9', '2026-08-26', 4944, 'The carried market reference is approximately $372B for FY2027 Data Center revenue, at rung 3 ($350B to below $400B).'), ('v9', '2026-08-27', 5139, 'The carried market reference is approximately $372 billion, on rung 3 (Neutral: $350 billion to below $400 billion).')] [('v9', '2026-09-13', 7770, 'Approximately $372 billion, the market reference carried from the prior conclusion; this sits at rung 3.'), ('v9', '2026-09-14', 7939, 'The market reference carried from the previous judgement is approximately $372 billion, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).'), ('v9', '2026-09-16', 8206, 'The approximately $414 billion FY2027 expectation carried from the previous conclusion is the reference; it sits at rung 2 ($400 billion to below $450 billion).'), ('v9', '2026-09-20', 8737, 'The market reference supplied by the previous conclusion is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2 ($400 billion to below $450 billion).')]\\ncontrol 3 v41 2026-09-20 [Decimal('38'), Decimal('39'), Decimal('40')] [('38', False), ('39', False), ('40', False)] [Decimal('20'), Decimal('40')]\\n3 {'all-USD': 641, 'parsed-USD': 641, 'at least one new-USD': 463, 'previous': 731, 'parsed-ref': 699, 'lead no nearby-USD': 283, 'lead no quote nearby-USD': 286, 'no nearby at all-USD': 25, 'hidden': 495, 'all-percent': 108, 'parsed-percent': 108, 'lead no nearby-percent': 36, 'lead no quote nearby-percent': 56, 'no nearby at all-percent': 3, 'at least one new-percent': 45, 'own and no evidence': 8} {'all-USD': '2026-07-01', 'parsed-USD': '2026-07-01', 'at least one new-USD': '2026-07-01', 'previous': '2026-07-02', 'parsed-ref': '2026-07-02', 'lead no nearby-USD': '2026-07-03', 'lead no quote nearby-USD': '2